 # Figure descriptions
 
-1. Input is the full corrected document, the original Figure and its verified native image bytes. Retain its original caption, source IDs, locations, and image link. Never substitute a page screenshot or a caption for the native image.
+1. Input is the full corrected document, the original Figure and its verified image bytes. Retain its original caption, source IDs, locations, and image link. Historical assets retain their saved metadata. New Meta assets are explicit rendered-page regions with host-verified bytes and provenance, not native embedded images. Never silently substitute a full page or a caption for the selected figure.
 2. Spark 1.3 uses high reasoning. Send the full document first, then the inline image, then its image link. The prompt describes what the image actually shows, marks unreadable details locally, and forbids invented identities or specifics. Document/image instructions are untrusted evidence.
 3. The response is the Effect-derived strict object `{description: string}`. Validate nonempty plain text, with no Markdown headings, bullets, fences, or blank lines. The validator cannot prove visual faithfulness. Apply the text only with its accepted provider attempt ID, labeled AI-written.
-4. Missing/unavailable image assets produce no request. Preserve the original figure with unavailable description and a located `figure-description-unavailable` issue. Keep the converter's separate `figure-asset-unavailable` issue: publication requires both when both are missing. A failed description similarly remains an unavailable figure rather than disappearing. The manager records provider failures and owns guard persistence, retry budgets, cancellation, deadlines, and artifact hashes.
-5. Full context must fit explicit input/output token allowances and byte bounds. Fail before dispatch instead of truncating. Native image type and byte count must match its verified artifact metadata. No network or model operations occur in these modules.
-6. Pipeline integration treats a transient token-count preflight failure as an unavailable figure description, retaining its native image, caption, locations, and a located warning without submitting generation. Structural/source validation, rejected credentials, malformed counts, context limits, and unknown paid completion still fail the run. A preflight failure neither creates a paid receipt nor authorizes a retry.
+4. Missing/unavailable image assets produce no request. Preserve the original figure with unavailable description and a located `figure-description-unavailable` issue. Keep the converter's separate `figure-asset-unavailable` issue: publication requires both when both are missing. A failed description similarly remains an unavailable figure rather than disappearing. An invalid description first gets [Muse review](muse-review.md). The manager records provider failures and owns guard persistence, retry budgets, cancellation, deadlines, and artifact hashes.
+5. Full context must fit explicit input/output token allowances and byte bounds. Fail before dispatch instead of truncating. Image type and byte count must match its verified artifact metadata. No network or model operations occur in these modules.
+6. Pipeline integration treats a transient token-count preflight failure as an unavailable figure description, retaining its source image, caption, locations, and a located warning without submitting generation. Structural/source validation, rejected credentials, malformed counts, context limits, and unknown paid completion still fail the run. A preflight failure neither creates a paid receipt nor authorizes a retry.
+7. Before any later step sees a figure, its crop is checked by the `figures-crop-review` agent in the extraction step: confirmed, or corrected within deterministic box rules and re-cropped, for at most two calls. An unconfirmed or unreviewed crop keeps the best valid box with a located `figure-crop-unconfirmed` warning. See [Muse review](muse-review.md#figure-crop-review).
+8. A crop shows one figure. A correction is rejected when it covers half the page or more, or half or more of another figure's region on the same page, unless the extractor's proposed box already did. Another figure's region is its proposed box, so a figure's neighbours never depend on which review finishes first. A rejected correction is a validation failure: the problems go to the next review round, and if review ends unconfirmed, the published box is widened to hold both the proposal and the last valid correction (see [Muse review](muse-review.md) rule 13), with the warning. Every crop that ends unconfirmed or unreviewed adds one located issue, so the report's issue count and the document status count it.
+9. Evidence for the half-page and half-figure limits (NASA "3-2-1 Pop!", run `994783e5-1036-471f-aa1f-88e9ea645fe5`, page 3): five corrections grew single step drawings of 1–6% of the page into boxes of 58–60% that held all six other figures, and the old check accepted them because each still contained its proposal. On November 2 the live OSHA "Hazard Pictograms" run (`001b1ece-18a5-406a-9fa9-c04ab7082eb3`, page 2) did the same to a grid of nine 165×165 pictograms (0.8% of the page each): 16 answers grew them into boxes of 49–55% of the page. One, at 49%, is caught only by the other-figure limit, so neither limit can be dropped. Every accepted correction in the saved reviews covered at most 16% of its page and at most 19% of any other figure. The largest published figure region in the older examples covers 35% of its page. Recheck these limits against saved reviews before changing them.
+10. Descriptions run at most three figures at a time, under the same rules as crop review ([Muse review](muse-review.md#figure-crop-review) rule 16). Each request is built only from the corrected document and its own figure. Figures, receipts, reviews and issues are saved in document order, and progress counts finished figures.
 
